she was amazing  she cared so deeply for what she did and  she cared so deeply about  the residents that she looked after and  ultimately she paid the price for them  patricia crowhurst worked as a carer for  over  20 years it was a job she loved  but her family is worried she put  herself at risk  i think there needs to be some form of  investigation as to why so many people  have  died and why that it's being allowed  why so many health care workers and  nhs staff and nurses  everybody some covid deaths could be  investigated  by the health and safety executive which  enforces  workplace safety sky news has learned  the hsc has received 91  fatal disease reports relating to health  and social care workers 26 of these are  in local authority care settings  for the hsc these are huge numbers put  in context  last year it investigated a total of 147  workplace deaths experts say the hsc  must decide whether employers  failed to protect their staff was that  risk reasonably  foreseeable did we select the correct  ppe  did we have enough staff were they  properly supervised there  are there are many many things to  consider what if the workplace says  we just couldn't get our hands on the  right ppe the health and safety of staff  is the responsibility of the employer  health safety and welfare and then  they've got to do everything that's  reasonably practicable to safeguard  their staff  the hsc has the power to fine or  prosecute  nhs trusts or care providers in breach  of the rules  i've spoken to one bereaved family  who've been  contacted by the hsc they didn't want to  talk on camera  but tell me they welcome the hsc's  involvement  because they've got serious misgivings  about the level of protection  offered by their relatives employer  they're not alone many families are now  starting  private legal proceedings provision of  ppe  is one of the big issues one of the big  questions that we're being asked about  were they provided with proper ppe  was it fit for purpose and if not why  was that the case  and if we're able to establish that the  ppe simply wasn't there and  should have been that may be the first  hurdle the families need to get over  the department of health and social care  says it's taken steps to remind  employers of their responsibilities  in a statement a spokesperson said the  safety of our nhs and social care staff  is  paramount and employers should follow  their legal duty  to report the deaths of any staff who  die as a result of exposure to  coronavirus  we've been working around the clock to  ensure ppe  is delivered as quickly as possible to  those on the front line  patricia crowhurst is one of the 250  health and social care workers  sky news understands have died with  covid  the hsc says it's committed to getting  an accurate picture  of those deaths and expects more will be  reported  in the coming weeks laura banduck sky  news
 

